C. Correct. A single session extension may sometimes be clinically appropriate. The more serious concern is the developing pattern of repeated special treatment combined with secrecy and avoidance of professional review. Concealment may indicate that the therapist recognizes the arrangement cannot be easily justified. It also prevents consultation, weakens accountability, and may signal that objectivity or personal involvement is beginning to affect clinical decisions.
