C. Correct. Determining who primarily benefits helps distinguish a client-centered therapeutic action from conduct that may be serving the therapist’s emotional, social, financial, or personal interests. A crossing is generally less concerning when it serves an identifiable client need, is limited, preserves objectivity, and is monitored. Concern increases when the therapist gains companionship, admiration, emotional support, money, control, approval, or romantic gratification.
