C. Correct. Client vulnerability can affect how freely a client responds to a therapist’s request or boundary change. A client experiencing trauma, crisis, dependency, isolation, or fear of losing treatment may feel unable to refuse even when the client appears to agree. The therapist should therefore consider whether the crossing could increase dependency, create pressure, interfere with autonomy, or expose the client to exploitation.
