C. Correct. The overlap is difficult to avoid and declining treatment could significantly limit the client’s access to care. The therapist should therefore determine whether the risks can be responsibly managed. A comprehensive response includes identifying both roles, evaluating confidentiality, power, objectivity, and conflicting loyalties, planning for public contact, limiting unnecessary personal interaction, documenting the safeguards, seeking consultation when useful, and monitoring the arrangement over time. Referral becomes appropriate when confidentiality cannot be protected, objectivity cannot be maintained, roles become incompatible, or the client feels pressured or confused.
