	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist begins providing services without telling the client what the fee will be or how the fee will be calculated. The therapist explains the charges after several sessions have already occurred. Which statement is most accurate?',
		'option_a'       => 'California requires the fee or basis for calculating the fee to be disclosed before treatment begins',
		'option_b'       => 'The disclosure is timely because the client received the information before treatment ended',
		'option_c'       => 'Fee disclosure is optional when the therapist charges a customary community rate',
		'option_d'       => 'The therapist may postpone disclosure whenever insurance might eventually reimburse the client',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Correct. The workbook identifies failure to disclose the fee or the basis for calculating the fee before treatment begins as a California unprofessional-conduct concern. Clients should understand the financial terms of treatment before services start so they can make informed decisions about participation. Relevant information may include the session fee, how charges are calculated, payment expectations, cancellation policies, additional charges, insurance billing, and how future fee changes will be communicated.
B. Incorrect. Disclosure after treatment has already begun is not equivalent to disclosure beforehand. The client incurred financial obligations without knowing the terms in advance.
C. Incorrect. A fee does not become exempt from disclosure merely because it appears reasonable or customary. The concern is whether the client received timely and understandable information.
D. Incorrect. Possible insurance reimbursement does not eliminate the therapist’s responsibility to clarify the fee arrangement. The client still needs to understand personal financial obligations and how charges will be calculated.
CTA Exam Strategy: When a question states that the fee was explained after treatment began, recognize a direct California-law issue. Do not treat it merely as a general communication problem.',
