C. Correct. The therapist would become both the client’s clinician and customer. The therapist may need to evaluate the client’s work, negotiate materials and deadlines, or address dissatisfaction—all of which could affect the clinical relationship. The exchange may also be difficult to value fairly. The client may feel pressure to accept because of financial need or fear that refusal could affect treatment. Safer alternatives, including a payment plan, reduced fee, community resources, or affordable referral, should be considered.
