C. Incorrect. Client participation in arranging the loan does not make it professionally appropriate. The therapist initiated the request and remains responsible for the boundary.
D. Correct. Borrowing from a current client places the therapist’s personal financial interests directly inside the therapeutic relationship. The client may feel unable to decline, may hope the loan will create special status, or may fear that refusal will affect treatment. The arrangement also creates creditor-debtor roles, emotional indebtedness, divided loyalties, and substantial risk of exploitation and impaired judgment. The client’s financial ability to lend does not resolve these concerns.
