	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist does the following: Begins treatment before disclosing how fees will be calculated. Pays a colleague for each psychotherapy client sent to the practice. Separately pays that colleague for documented clinical consultation actually provided. Which analysis is most accurate?',
		'option_a'       => 'All three arrangements are acceptable because licensed clinicians are involved',
		'option_b'       => 'Only the delayed fee disclosure creates a concern',
		'option_c'       => 'Delayed fee disclosure and payment merely for client referrals raise direct California concerns, while reasonable payment for professional services actually provided is analytically different',
		'option_d'       => 'The referral payments are acceptable as long as clients are not told about them',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Professional licensure does not eliminate fee-disclosure or referral-compensation requirements. Licensed clinicians remain subject to the statute.
B. Incorrect. The per-client referral payment creates a separate concern because the compensation is tied to sending clients rather than providing legitimate professional services.
C. Correct. California Business and Professions Code section 4982 identifies as unprofessional conduct failing to disclose the fee or basis for calculating the fee before treatment begins. It also addresses paying, accepting, or soliciting compensation for professional client referrals and distinguishes that from compensation connected to professional counseling services actually provided. The payment for documented clinical consultation is therefore different from a payment made solely because a client was sent to the therapist. The actual purpose of the compensation—not the label placed on it—controls the analysis. The attached workbook makes the same distinction between legitimate services and payment merely for a referral.
D. Incorrect. Concealing the payment does not make it permissible. Secrecy would add further concern and would not change the arrangement’s underlying purpose.
CTA Exam Strategy: When a California financial question appears, separate the issues: Was the fee or fee basis disclosed before treatment began? Is the payment for a legitimate service—or merely for sending a client? Is the financial conduct honest, transparent, and free from exploitation?',
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A client gives the therapist an inexpensive keychain and says, “Now you will remember that I am your most important client.” What should concern the therapist most?',
